                                div.special-issue
                                    h3
                                        a(href="#") Special Issue: Scientific Balloon Technologies
                                    div.item-content
                                        div.item-content__text
                                            | Scientific ballooning facilitates high quality science investigations across multiple disciplines, including astrophysics, planetary, and solar science. Though the payloads differ, there are commonalities in the technologies for the platform and operations. This special issue highlights these shared technologies, discussing their state of the art for specific payloads and balloon programs.
                                        div.item-content__image
                                            img(src="https://www.worldscientific.com/sda/1523/jai0602.jpg")

                            div.journal-featured__list.section
                                h3.section__header.border-bottom Featured Articles
                                ul.rlist.lot
                                    li.journal-featured__item
                                        h3
                                            a(href="#") Bifrost: A Python/C++ Framework for High-Throughput Stream Processing in Astronomy
                                        span.meta Miles D. Cranmer, Benjamin R. Barsdell, Danny C. Price et al.
                                        span.meta J. Astron. Instrum., 06 (2017) 1750007
                                        div.item-content
                                            div.item-content__text
                                                | Bifrost combines a high-level Python interface with highly efficient reconfigurable data transport and a library of computing blocks for CPU and GPU processing. The framework is generalizable, but initially it emphasizes the needs of high-throughput radio astronomy pipelines, such as the ability to process data buffers as if they were continuous streams, the capacity to partition processing into distinct data sequences (e.g. separate observations), and the ability to extract specific intervals from buffered data. Computing blocks in the library are designed for applications such as interferometry, pulsar dedispersion and timing, and transient search pipelines.
                                            div.item-content__image
                                                img(src="https://www.worldscientific.com/sda/1523/S2251171717500076.jpg")
                                    li.journal-featured__item
                                        h3
                                            a(href="#") Simulation and Testing of a Linear Array of Modified Four-Square Feed Antennas for the Tianlai Cylindrical Radio Telescope
                                        span.meta Aleksander J. Cianciara, Christopher J. Anderson, Xuelei Chen et al.
                                        span.meta J. Astron. Instrum., 06 (2017) 1750003
                                        div.item-content
                                            div.item-content__text
                                                | A wide bandwidth, dual polarized, modified four-square antenna is presented as a feed antenna for radio astronomical measurements. A linear array of these antennas is used as a line-feed for cylindrical reflectors for Tianlai, a radio interferometer designed for 21 cm intensity mapping. Simulations of the feed antenna beam patterns and scattering parameters are compared to experimental results at multiple frequencies across the 650-1420MHz range. Simulations of the beam patterns of the combined feed array/reflector are presented as well.
                                            div.item-content__image
                                                img(src="https://www.worldscientific.com/sda/1523/S2251171717500039.jpg")


